          <p>
            Built because a spare portrait monitor next to the main display was sitting idle during gaming sessions.
            Portrait Stats fills it with live CPU, GPU, RAM, drive, and FPS telemetry — auto-detecting whichever
            connected display is actually in portrait orientation rather than assuming a fixed monitor index.
          </p>
          <div class="project-columns">
            <div>
              <h4>What It Shows</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>CPU and GPU usage, temperature, clocks, and power draw; motherboard fan RPM for every live header, fastest first, with editable names since most boards won't say which one is the CPU fan.</li>
                <li>Live FPS via RTSS shared memory, top processes by CPU and GPU usage, vendor badges, radial gauges, and sparkline history.</li>
                <li>C: drive temperature, auto-detecting whichever physical disk actually backs the system drive.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Design Notes</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>A self-contained release build — no separate .NET runtime install needed.</li>
                <li>Requires elevation for LibreHardwareMonitorLib's sensor access; auto-starts via a Scheduled Task instead of a normal startup entry, so it launches elevated without a UAC prompt every login.</li>
                <li>Fan RPM is read by briefly launching a separate helper process on a timer rather than holding a sensor connection open continuously — documented as a deliberate architecture trade-off, not an oversight.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </section>
